f 1062 #uuid "02da344d-81c6-58ac-8ecb-af97afe0c3c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055756 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055844 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055844 #edb/ref 1061 625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055844 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ac48d370-ba29-5343-bf37-4b82f3611773" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055844 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186055978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055845 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055845 #edb/ref 1061 626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055845 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ea42d926-8045-5d22-9aa7-2fd3a3f233c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055845 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055846 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055846 #edb/ref 1061 629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055846 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8893f4d9-c611-591e-80a2-799835854aae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055846 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055847 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055847 #edb/ref 1061 631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055847 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "41c0eff0-f259-5568-b570-bafddf204af8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055847 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055848 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055848 #edb/ref 1061 633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055848 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "421f20ea-c000-57af-973a-3496371dd497" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055848 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186055983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055849 #edb/ref 1061 634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055849 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "814ed39b-61cd-5eb5-92ed-72ff4dbe9a1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055849 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055928 #edb/ref 1064 "| Reading Time (minutes) | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $0 \\leq x \\lt 20$ | $5$ |\n| $20 \\leq x \\lt 40$ | $10$ |\n| $40 \\leq x \\lt 60$ | $8$ |\n| $60 \\leq x \\lt 80$ | $7$ |\n\nWe wish to find $\\overline x$, the mean time library members spend in the reading room.\n\nSince we don't know exactly how long each member spent in the reading room, finding a precise answer for the mean is impossible. Therefore, the best we can do is *estimate* the mean.\n\nTo estimate the mean, we first estimate the mean time members of each class spent in the reading room. This is the same as calculating the *midpoint* of each class.\n\nTo find the midpoint of an interval\n\n$$\na \\leq x < b\n$$\n\nwe take the mean of the endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{midpoint} = \\dfrac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nFor example, we can find the midpoint of the first class\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x < 20\n$$\n\nwhich we'll denote as $m_1$, as follows:\n\n$$\nm_1 = \\dfrac{0+20}{2} = 10\n$$\n\nContinuing this for the second, third, and fourth classes, we get the following table:\n\n| Reading Time (minutes) | $f_i$ | $m_i$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $0 \\leq x \\lt 20$ | $5$ | $10$ |\n| $20 \\leq x \\lt 40$ | $10$ | $30$ |\n| $40 \\leq x \\lt 60$ | $8$ | $50$ |\n| $60 \\leq x \\lt 80$ | $7$ | $70$ |\n\nFor notational convenience, we've denoted $f_i$ as the frequency associated with the $i$th class.\n\nNext, we wish to estimate the total time members in each class spent in the reading room. To do this, we multiply the midpoint of each class with the respective frequency:\n\n| Reading Time (minutes) | $f_i$ | $m_i$ | $f_i\\cdot m_i$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0 \\leq x \\lt 20$ | $5$ | $10$ | $50$ |\n| $20 \\leq x \\lt 40$ | $10$ | $30$ | $300$ |\n| $40 \\leq x \\lt 60$ | $8$ | $50$ | $400$ |\n| $60 \\leq x \\lt 80$ | $7$ | $70$ | $490$ |\n\nFinally, to estimate the mean $\\overline x$, we sum the values in the last column (our estimate of the total time members spent in the reading room) and divide by $30$ (the total number of observations).\n\n$$\nx^(―) \\mid \\approx \\frac{50 + 300 + 400 + 490}{30}; = \\frac{1240}{30}; = 41.333…; \\approx 41.3\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the mean time spent in the reading room is approximately $41.3$ minutes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055928 #edb/ref 1065 10897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055928 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "03affb4a-4039-53fc-8f9a-8c2c8718cc8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055928 #edb/ref 1067 "Estimating a Mean" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055929 #edb/ref 1064 "If we have a data set that consists of the points $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$, then the variance, denoted by $\\sigma^2$, is defined as\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2= \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$ is the mean of the data set.\n\nGiven a frequency table containing continuous data split into $K$ classes, the variance can be estimated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2_n \\approx \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^K f_i \\cdot (m_{i} - \\overline x)^2\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f_i$ is the frequency of the $i$th class,\n- $m_i$ is the midpoint of the $i$th class,\n- $n$ is the total number of observations, and\n- $\\overline x$ is the mean.\n\nWhen *estimating* a variance, we often divide by $n-1$ instead of $n$ (the reasons for this will be explained in future lessons). We write $\\sigma_n^2$ here to avoid confusion about which denominator we're dividing by.\n\nAlso, note that if $\\overline x$ is unknown, it must be estimated using the previously described method.\n\nLet's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055929 #edb/ref 1065 10898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055929 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "deda1ed9-9639-5a76-9f4e-ff548d017862" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055929 #edb/ref 1067 "Estimating a Variance" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055930 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a frequency table containing continuous data split into $K$ classes, the mean can be estimated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overline x \\approx \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^K f_i \\cdot m_{i}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f_i$ is the frequency of the $i$th class,\n- $m_i$ is the midpoint of the $i$th class, and\n- $n$ is the total number of observations." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055930 #edb/ref 1065 10899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055930 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a1fb09c8-7bf1-5b0b-99a3-5c2862f8651d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055930 #edb/ref 1067 "A Summary of Mean Estimation for Continuous Grouped Data" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055931 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's once again consider the data collected by the librarian, shown below.\n\n| Reading Time (minutes) | $f_i$ | $m_i$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $0 \\leq x \\lt 20$ | $5$ | $10$ |\n| $20 \\leq x \\lt 40$ | $10$ | $30$ |\n| $40 \\leq x \\lt 60$ | $8$ | $50$ |\n| $60 \\leq x \\lt 80$ | $7$ | $70$ |\n\nEarlier, we found that\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}\\approx 41.3333\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places. Also, we have $n=30$ observations, which equals the sum of the $f_i$ column.\n\nLet's estimate the variance of this data set to one decimal place using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2_n \\approx \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^K f_i \\cdot (m_{i} - \\overline x)^2\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the deviations of each midpoint from the estimated mean, rounding to four decimal places.\n\n| Reading Time (minutes) | $f_i$ | $m_i$ | $m_i - \\overline x$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0 \\leq x \\lt 20$ | $5$ | $10$ | $-31.3333$ |\n| $20 \\leq x \\lt 40$ | $10$ | $30$ | $-11.3333$ |\n| $40 \\leq x \\lt 60$ | $8$ | $50$ | $8.6667$ |\n| $60 \\leq x \\lt 80$ | $7$ | $70$ | $28.6667$ |\n\nNext, we square each deviation:\n\n| Reading Time (minutes) | $f_i$ | $m_i$ | $m_i - \\overline x$ | $(m_i - \\overline x)^2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0 \\leq x \\lt 20$ | $5$ | $10$ | $-31.3333$ | $981.7757$ |\n| $20 \\leq x \\lt 40$ | $10$ | $30$ | $-11.3333$ | $128.4437$ |\n| $40 \\leq x \\lt 60$ | $8$ | $50$ | $8.6667$ | $75.1117$ |\n| $60 \\leq x \\lt 80$ | $7$ | $70$ | $28.6667$ | $821.7797$ |\n\nThen, we multiply each squared deviation by the respective frequency:\n\n| Reading Time (minutes) | $f_i$ | $m_i$ | $m_i - \\overline x$ | $(m_i - \\overline x)^2$ | $f_i\\cdot (m_i - \\overline x)^2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0 \\leq x \\lt 20$ | $5$ | $10$ | $-31.3333$ | $981.7757$ | $4908.8785$ |\n| $20 \\leq x \\lt 40$ | $10$ | $30$ | $-11.3333$ | $128.4437$ | $1284.4370$ |\n| $40 \\leq x \\lt 60$ | $8$ | $50$ | $8.6667$ | $75.1117$ | $600.8936$ |\n| $60 \\leq x \\lt 80$ | $7$ | $70$ | $28.6667$ | $821.7797$ | $5752.4579$ |\n\nFinally, we sum the values in the last column and divide by $30$ (the total number of observations).\n\n$$\nσ_{n}^{2} \\mid \\approx \\frac{4908.8785 + 1284.4370 + 600.8936 + 5752.4579}{30}; \\approx 418.2222; \\approx 418.2\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nFinally, we can find an estimate of the standard deviation by taking the square root:\n\n$$\n\\sigma_n \\approx \\sqrt{418.2222} \\approx 20.5\\,\\textrm{mins}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055931 #edb/ref 1065 10901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055931 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "549ee1a2-da04-59f6-b46d-81905fbe3d1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055931 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055962 #edb/ref 1064 "For a function $f(x)$, we can express the derivative as $f'(x)$.\n\nRecall that we can also express the derivative using the **differential operator**, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nWe can use the differential operator while computing the derivative, too. For example, the power rule can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\left(x^n \\right) = nx^{n-1}\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\big(\\cdots\\big)\n$$\n\njust means that we need to differentiate whatever is inside $\\big(\\cdots\\big)$ with respect to $x$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055962 #edb/ref 1065 2647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055962 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "537fb074-19a0-542c-9a84-c4542e26b33f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055962 #edb/ref 1067 "The Differential Operator" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055964 #edb/ref 1064 "It's possible to perform arithmetic with functions, just like with numbers. Shown below are the four arithmetic operations, addition $(+)$, subtraction $(-)$, multiplication $(\\cdot)$, and division $(/)$, applied to the functions\n\n$$\nf(x) = 4x + 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ng(x) = x - 2\n$$\n\n- **Addition**\n$\\begin{aligned} (f + g)(x) &= f(x) + g(x) \\\\ &= (4x + 1) + (x - 2) \\\\ &= 4x + 1 + x - 2 \\\\ &= 5x - 1 \\end{aligned}$\n- **Subtraction**\n$(f - g)(x)|= f(x) - g(x); = (4x + 1) - (x - 2); = 4x + 1 - x + 2; = 3x + 3$\n- **Multiplication**\n$(fg)(x)|= f(x)g(x); = (4x + 1)(x - 2); = 4x^{2} - 8x + x - 2; = 4x^{2} - 7x - 2$\n- **Division**\n$(f/g)(x)|= (f(x))/(g(x)); = \\frac{4x + 1}{x - 2}$\n\n**Note:** In the case of division, we must have that\n\n$$\ng(x)\\neq 0\n$$\n\nso that we avoid dividing by zero." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055964 #edb/ref 1065 2751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055964 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c656d962-19ad-5e04-b117-fa8d0c9034b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055964 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055968 #edb/ref 1064 "Multiplying a purely imaginary number by a real number is similar to multiplying two monomials.\n\nFor example, we can multiply the purely imaginary number $3 \\textrm{i}$ by the real number $6$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot 3\\text{i} & =(6\\cdot 3)\\text{i}=18\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we can multiply a complex number by a real number using the distributive property.\n\nFor example, we can multiply the complex number $2-3\\textrm{i}$ by the real number $3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n3(2-3\\textrm{i}) &= 3\\cdot2 + 3\\cdot(-3\\textrm{i}) \\[3pt]\n\t\t&= 6 + 3\\cdot (-3)\\textrm{i} \\[3pt]\n\t\t&= 6 -9\\textrm{i}\n\t}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055968 #edb/ref 1065 2789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055968 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4e54cae3-6a6c-50bb-a0d7-5ddc1fefaaf2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055968 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055970 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we wish to evaluate $\\log_{4}9$, but our calculator only has a key for the common logarithm $\\log$ (base $10$).\n\nIn such cases, we can use the **change of base formula**, which states that\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}\n$$\n\nIt effectively allows us to change a logarithm from one base, $a$, to another, $b$. Note that $X$, $a$, and $b$ have to be all greater than zero and that\n\n$$\na \\ne 1\n$$\n\n$$\nb \\ne 1\n$$\n\nUsing this formula, we can rewrite the logarithm from base $a=4$ to base $b=10$, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{4} 9 &= \\frac{\\log 9}{\\log 4} \\\\\n&\\approx \\frac{0.954242}{0.602059} \\\\\n&= 1.585\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055970 #edb/ref 1065 2797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055970 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bb104b87-e850-5cff-9826-fdd61c0287d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055970 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055971 #edb/ref 1064 "When we're asked to evaluate the sum of two functions at a particular point, there are two methods that we can use. We could either evaluate both functions at the point and sum the results. Or, we could sum the two functions and then evaluate the result at the point.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the $(f+g)(1)$ for\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^2 -1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ng(x) = 2x-3\n$$\n\nThe notation $(f+g)(x)$ means the sum of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(f + g)(1)$, we find $f(1)$ and $g(1)$, and then add the results:\n\nFirst, we find $f(1)$ and $g(1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1) &= (1)^{2} - 1 = 0 \\\\\ng(1) &= 2(1) - 3 =-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we add the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f + g)(1) &= f(1) + g(1) \\\\\n&= 0 + (-1) \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(f+g)(x)$ by adding the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f + g)(x) &= (x^{2} - 1) + (2x - 3) \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 2x - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(f + g)(1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f + g)(1) &= (1)^{2} + 2(1) - 4 =-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth these methods work fine, but method $1$ is usually faster.\n\nFinally, note that we can evaluate the difference between two functions using the same methods." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055971 #edb/ref 1065 2834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055971 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "74671b66-6f9b-59a5-96e2-aeb7ca503afe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055971 #edb/ref 1067 "Evaluating the Sum or Difference of Two Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
